                     <incipit xml:lang="ar">
                        <locus target="#2v">-2v</locus>بِسْمِ اللّهِ الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ للّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ مَلِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ<note>1:1</note>
                     </incipit>
                     <explicit xml:lang="ar">
                        <locus target="#40v">-40v</locus>تِلْكَ أُمَّةٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ ۖ لَهَا مَا كَسَبَتْ وَلَكُم مَّا كَسَبْتُمْ ۖ وَلَا تُسْئلُونَ عَمَّا كَانُوا يَعْمَلُونَ<note>2:134</note>
                     </explicit>
                     <note>1:1-2:134.
Some words are divided into two different lines.
The “ḏāl” is written with the dot inside. Sometimes the catchword is only the first part of the first word in the next page, sometimes there is a repeated word and the catchword is the second word of the next page. In the folio 5v the last word is divided and the catchword is only the second part of the word. 
The name of the scribe appears in mss. 4521D (37r) and 4521X (1v, 45v). The note stating that he is actually the scribe is in 4521X (45v).  

</note>
